# Push every change to GitHub

After **any** meaningful app change (tick/stamp visuals, packaging, verifier, UI, trust roots, CI), do this before ending the turn:

1. Commit the relevant files on `main` (or the active feature branch).
2. **Merge to `main` and `git push origin main`** immediately — do not wait for the user to ask.
3. Confirm GitHub Actions `release-deb.yml` published a new **GitHub Release** `.deb` (this is what the in-app **Update** button installs).
4. Tell the user the new version tag (e.g. 1.0.35) so employees can click **Check for update**.

## Global deploy (required every time)

The in-app updater always downloads **latest** from:

`https://github.com/ramchandragada/SignVerifierForLinux/releases/latest`

So a change is **not done** until it is on `main` and a new release asset exists. Feature-branch-only fixes never reach other PCs.

## Packaging / .deb builds

- After a local `.deb` build **or** a packaging fix, ensure the matching source change is **committed and pushed** so GitHub Releases stays current for employees.
- Prefer letting `.github/workflows/release-deb.yml` publish the universal `.deb` on push to `main`; still push the source so CI runs.

## Do not skip

- Never leave verified fixes only on the local machine.
- Never treat “build finished locally” as done without a remote update when the change belongs in the product.
- Never leave an app fix only on a PR branch if users need it via the Update button.
